\DOC LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances the symbolic execution of an {eventually} goal across an inlined
modular-subtraction subroutine, replacing the current state variable with a
successor state and recording the subtraction's specification as a new
assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index selector ({0} in all observed
traces); the second is a {string list} naming, in order, the destination buffer
and the two operand buffers of the subtraction, which determine which stored
value is produced and which stored values are subtracted. Applied to a goal
whose conclusion is an {eventually} claim about machine state {s} and whose
assumptions describe {s}, it yields a single subgoal about a fresh successor
state {s'}:
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ================================  LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC 0 [dest; op1; op2]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Every assumption mentioning the old state — program-counter value,
code-loaded fact, register contents, memory-block contents, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state — is discharged and re-added in
{A'} with {s'} in place of {s}: the program counter is advanced, the
{MAYCHANGE} relation is extended with the destination memory block, and an
assumption giving the destination buffer's new contents is added. One further
assumption is added stating that, under the hypothesis that both operands are
reduced, the destination value equals the difference of the operands taken as
an integer remainder modulo the field prime. Assumptions not referring to the
state are left unchanged and keep their relative order.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} claim with a
state-describing assumption list.

\ENDDOC
